{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/ScienceSimplified1/posts/a-country-under-six-decades-of-sanctions-built-something-american-pharma-hadnt-a/122334393194010907/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/ScienceSimplified1/posts/a-country-under-six-decades-of-sanctions-built-something-american-pharma-hadnt-a/122334393194010907/","title":"Science Simplified","description":"A country under six decades of sanctions built something American pharma hadn't: a therapeutic vaccine that extends survival for advanced lung cancer patients. It's called CIMAvax-EGF, and Cubans get...","language":"en","text":"A country under six decades of sanctions built something American pharma hadn't: a therapeutic vaccine that extends survival for advanced lung cancer patients. It's called CIMAvax-EGF, and Cubans get it for free.\n\nThe vaccine doesn't attack the tumor directly. It targets EGF — a growth protein the cancer drinks like fuel. The vaccine trains the immune system to strip EGF from the blood, essentially starving the tumor. Trials show meaningful survival extension in specific patient groups, and it's approved across Cuba and several other countries, with studies run as far as Japan.\n\nThe controversy is pure geopolitics. The US embargo makes American access legally tangled — Roswell Park in Buffalo ran a rare FDA-approved trial, the only path around the wall, but progress moved slowly. An American lung cancer patient generally cannot fly to Havana and come home with treatment without navigating sanctions law.\n\nMeanwhile, roughly 350 Americans die of lung cancer every single day. A survival-extending option exists 90 miles offshore, kept at bay not by science, but by politics written in 1962.\n\nShould a trade embargo decide which cancer treatments a dying American can reach?\n\nSource: Center of Molecular Immunology Havana / Roswell Park Trial, ongoing\n\nAll reactions:\n\n1 share\n\nLike\n\nComment","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1764.2988999996305,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}